{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A WAL has explicit checkpoint records; token-budget exhaustion may not leave a marker.","Database recovery can replay; append-only reasoning logs must not replay.","Model state is probabilistic and harder to fingerprint than deterministic computation."],"overturn_conditions":["If events are self-describing and causally linked by content hash, even a logical mid-unit stop preserves identity because the next event simply references the last complete one.","If the system supports partial-event resumption (streaming append), the mid-write case may be safe under a different protocol."],"shared_structure":["A checkpointed process interrupted by external resource exhaustion (token budget ≈ CPU time limit).","Resume requires a valid checkpoint marker and unchanged environment.","Append-only log ≈ write-ahead log (WAL) in databases."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The WAL discipline of 'never overwrite, only append' transfers directly: resumption is safe iff the last committed record is complete.","The 'fuzzy checkpoint' problem transfers: if the checkpoint is ambiguous, recovery must be conservative."]},"claim":"Resumption is safe if and only if the last recorded event in the log is well-formed and complete, and the causal preconditions (program, inputs, model-state checkpoint) are demonstrably unchanged at resumption time. The append-only identity is preserved because new events extend the same causal chain without rewriting any prior event.","counterconditions":["If the last event is partial or malformed (interrupted mid-write), resumption cannot locate a valid causal parent and must be refused.","If the program, input, or model checkpoint has changed since the run started, the causal fingerprint mismatches and resumed events would not belong to the same causal chain.","If the event-log format lacks a content-addressable hash linking events, there is no mechanism to verify causal continuity on resumption."],"mechanism":"An event log maintains append-only identity through a causal chain: each event references its predecessor. Token-budget exhaustion is an external interrupt, not a logical termination. If the interrupt fell between complete events (not mid-write), the last event's hash serves as a resume cursor. The resumption process reads this cursor, verifies the program/input/model fingerprint matches, and appends new events that reference the last committed event as their causal parent. No rewrite occurs, so append-only identity is intact.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"Resumption must be refused whenever token-budget exhaustion interrupts mid-event, because the partial event either has no well-defined identity (cannot be referenced as a causal parent) or must be rewritten to become complete (violating append-only). There is no safe recovery path for mid-event interruption under strict append-only semantics.","counterconditions":["If the system supports an 'abort marker' that can be appended after a partial event to tombstone it, resumption could proceed by appending a fresh event after the abort marker.","If events are byte-stream fragments rather than atomic records, partial writes are normal and resumption is always safe."],"mechanism":"An event in the log must be atomically committed: it has a beginning marker, content, an end marker, and a predecessor hash. If the token budget runs out between the beginning and end markers, the log contains a partial record. Any resumption faces a dilemma: (a) append a new event referencing the partial one—invalid because the partial event has no stable identity/hash; (b) rewrite the partial event to completion—violates append-only. Both paths corrupt identity, so refusal is the only responsible action.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"Append-only identity is never corrupted by resumption per se; corruption only occurs if the resumption re-evaluates or rewrites already-committed events. Therefore resumption is always permissible as long as the continuation starts strictly after the last committed event, regardless of whether the logical reasoning unit was complete.","counterconditions":["If downstream consumers assume that the event stream represents a complete, logically coherent trace, then a mid-unit resumption produces a semantically incoherent stream even though it is structurally valid append-only.","If the reasoning workflow contract requires that the event log be a valid transcript of a single uninterrupted run, then any resumption violates the workflow contract regardless of append-only preservation.","If causal identity includes 'single-run continuity' as a constitutive property, then resumption after interruption changes the identity of the whole."],"mechanism":"Append-only is a storage invariant, not a semantic invariant. Each event is committed atomically with a content hash and predecessor link. The causal chain is a linked list; appending a new node after any committed node preserves the list's integrity. The logical incompleteness of the reasoning (e.g., a chain-of-thought cut mid-argument) is a semantic property orthogonal to the append-only storage identity. The resumed event simply declares itself as a continuation after event N, and the log remains valid.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"claim":"Resumption is safe only if the system emitted an explicit 'barrier' or 'suspend' event before or at the point of token-budget exhaustion. Without a barrier, even well-formed events may be part of an incomplete logical unit (e.g., a multi-event inference step), and resumption could produce events that semantically depend on context that was never written to the log.","counterconditions":["If each event is fully self-contained and carries all necessary context (stateless reasoning), no barrier is needed and resumption is safe after any complete event.","If the system checkpoints full reasoning state (including hidden attention/memory state) to durable storage on every event, then the state is recoverable without a barrier."],"mechanism":"A reasoning run produces events in logical units that may span multiple atomic events (e.g., 'begin inference step', 'intermediate result', 'commit inference step'). Token-budget exhaustion may interrupt after 'intermediate result' but before 'commit inference step'. The log now contains a dangling logical unit. Resumption would append 'commit inference step' but the causal context (working memory, attention state) that informed the original step is lost. A barrier event would have captured this context. Without it, the resumed events are causally disconnected from the reasoning state that produced the prior events, corrupting identity.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"claim":"Resumption requires an exact causal fingerprint match: the triple (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) at resumption must equal the triple at run start. Any divergence means resumed events belong to a different causal identity, making the append-only log internally inconsistent—a single log containing events from two causally distinct runs.","counterconditions":["If the system explicitly models resumption as a new causal segment (e.g., a 'resume' event with its own fingerprint), then a different fingerprint is acceptable as long as it is declared.","If model state is nondeterministic by design (e.g., stochastic sampling), exact fingerprint matching is impossible and the criterion would forbid all resumption, which is too strict.","If the program is interpreted and minor implementation changes don't alter observable behavior, exact hash matching is overly brittle."],"mechanism":"The identity of an event stream is not merely 'the same log file' but 'the same causal origin.' An event's identity is determined by the causal process that generated it: (program, inputs, model state). If any component changes (e.g., model weights updated, program patched, input modified), the resumed events are causally alien to the pre-interruption events. Inserting them into the same append-only log creates a Frankenstein stream: structurally append-only but causally incoherent. The fingerprint check is the gatekeeper.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"Adjudication between rival criteria: the 'last complete event' criterion and the 'causal fingerprint' criterion are both necessary but neither is sufficient alone. The correct criterion is their conjunction: resumption is safe iff (1) the last event is well-formed and complete, AND (2) the causal fingerprint matches, AND (3) no intermediate event declares logical incompleteness (e.g., a 'begin' without a matching 'commit'). This conjunction subsumes both rivals and adds a logical-unit integrity check.","counterconditions":["If the system has no concept of multi-event logical units (every event is atomic and independent), criterion 3 is vacuous and the conjunction reduces to (1) AND (2).","If the system uses deterministic replay from the last event, criterion 2 may be redundant because replay reconstructs the same causal identity from the log itself.","If the reasoning envelope workflow explicitly permits multi-segment runs with explicit resume markers, criterion 3 is satisfied by the marker rather than by logical-unit completeness."],"mechanism":"The 'last complete event' criterion ensures structural integrity of the log. The 'causal fingerprint' criterion ensures causal identity continuity. The 'logical-unit integrity' criterion ensures semantic coherence: multi-event units must be complete or explicitly aborted. Token-budget exhaustion can violate any of the three independently: mid-write (violates 1), model update (violates 2), mid-inference-step (violates 3). Since each violation independently corrupts a different aspect of identity, all three must hold. The mechanism is a three-stage gate: structural check, fingerprint check, logical-unit check.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"SCR_001","holds_when":"last event is complete but causal preconditions changed","relation_hint":"structural integrity (complete event) is necessary but not sufficient without causal fingerprint","to_ref":"SCR_002","weakens_when":"model state is deterministic-replayable from log"},{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"SCR_001","holds_when":"multi-event logical units exist in the workflow","relation_hint":"structural and causal criteria are both necessary; logical-unit integrity adds a third independent gate","to_ref":"SCR_004","weakens_when":"all events are atomic and independent"}],"new_blocks":[],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does the reasoning-envelope-wf define a 'suspend' or 'checkpoint' event type, or is token-budget exhaustion an ungraceful interrupt with no protocol-level support?","related_refs":[]},{"question":"Are events in the reasoning log atomic records or byte-stream fragments? This determines whether mid-write interruption is even possible or whether 'partial event' is a meaningful concept.","related_refs":[]},{"question":"Is the model state at interruption recoverable from the event log alone (deterministic replay), or does it require an external checkpoint? This determines whether the causal fingerprint can be verified from the log or requires out-of-band state.","related_refs":[]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}